Wrong (B): Higher alpha is not always superior; it increases responsiveness at the cost of stability, which can amplify forecast error when demand fluctuations are random.
Wrong (C): Lower alpha is not always preferred either; when genuine trend shifts occur, low alpha produces sluggish forecasts that systematically lag actual demand.
Wrong (D): The two forecasts do not converge to the same value; different alpha values produce persistently different forecast paths as new actuals arrive.$EXPL$
